# Use operations from TypeScript

Operations are caller-visible durable workflows. Declare an Operation and its
top-level input, progress, output, error, update, and signal types in native IDL.
The caller selects the Operation once; that includes the complete lifecycle and
declared signals.

```ts
const operation = await client.reportsGenerate(input).start().orThrow();
const terminal = await operation.wait().orThrow();
```

Acceptance is persisted before the caller receives the reference. Retries with
the same invocation ID and input return the same operation; changed input
conflicts. Progress, signals, cancellation, and terminal state are durable. Live
updates are transient.

Execution is restartable and at least once. A replacement executor resumes from
persisted state after lease expiry, so application side effects must be
idempotent. Transfer staging is runtime-managed. An interrupted upload restarts
from byte zero; committed staged bytes survive executor replacement.
